      "writtenOn": "2023-01-05T04:00:15Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I don\u0027t think this will work, for multiple reasons.\n• This will happily create multiple NativeUidRangeConfig objects for the same network. If you have 2 enterprise preferences for 2 different profiles, they will come up with different lists of allowed UIDs for the same network, and you\u0027ll add them as a separate NativeUidRangeConfig. This is not what netd expects ; maybe we should have netd throw in this case in fact, but it should be disallowed to send a list with multiple objects for the same network.\n• This will block the wrong UIDs. Keep in mind that all UIDs not in the preference should not be restricted from using these networks, but this will restrict them. E.g. :\n    netid\u003d100 : wifi, non-enterprise\n    netid\u003d101 : cell, enterprise\n    Preference(profile\u003d2, mode\u003dBLOCKING, UIDs\u003d[2010005~2010008])\n\nYour code will say that only UIDs 2010005~2010008 have access to network 101, when that\u0027s absolutely incorrect : all UIDs should have access to network 101 (UIDs 0~2010004 and 2010009~9999999 have no restriction by the preference, and UIDs 2010005~2010008 are allowed because the network has the correct capabilities)\n\nYour code will also let UIDs 2010005~2010008 access network 100 because it won\u0027t send a NativeUidRangeConfig for netid 100, and that\u0027s also incorrect : they should be blocked.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 8727,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 8730,
        "endChar": 13
      },
      "revId": "42b3fa0c00425faeeeb5e7d7f73c678f74c083ac",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
